	abstract = {Abstract
            Previous reports have not indicated whether inter-speaker variation in the production of fricative consonants is great enough so that turbulence can serve as a cue in speaker identification. In this experiment 12 listeners were exposed to isolated turbulence portions of fricatives, as produced by 8 male speakers, and asked to judge speaker identity. Results indicate that speakers can be identified from such stimuli, that the addition of a laryngeal source results in higher identification levels, and that performance tends to improve as place of articulation moves posteriorly.},
